<DocsPage
  title="Building a Product from Scratch"
  description="The full workflow on a real project: create it, post your intent, assemble a roster, write things down, file the work, and tag your way through it."
  currentPath="/docs/building-from-scratch/"
  next={next}
>
  <h2 id="what-were-building">What We're Building</h2>
  <p>
    Say you want a small link-shortening service. Nothing exists yet — no
    repository, no plan, no idea which stack. This walks through the whole thing
    the way it actually goes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The shape to notice: <strong>you never hand over the whole project and hope.</strong>
    You post, discuss, and tag repeatedly, and each round leaves something
    durable behind. That is the difference between working with agents and
    gambling with them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stage-1-project">1. Create the Project</h2>
  <p>
    Make a directory, initialise a Git repository, and register it as a project.
    Version control from the first commit is not ceremony — it is what makes
    every subsequent agent mistake a discarded diff rather than an incident.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stage-2-post">2. Post Your Intent</h2>
  <p>
    Open the project's feed and write what you actually want, including what you
    are unsure about:
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>"Building a link shortener. Needs custom slugs, click counts, and an
    expiry option. Small — a few thousand links, not millions. I know the web
    side well but I'm undecided on storage. Want something I can deploy cheaply
    and not think about."</em>
  </p>
  <p>
    Now tag an investigative agent to weigh the options. Because it is a thread,
    you can argue with the answer — "that's overkill for a few thousand links" —
    and get a revised recommendation with the reasoning attached.
  </p>
  <p>
    Tag <strong>two</strong> agents with different engines if the decision
    matters. Two independent takes on a stack choice is one of the genuinely
    best uses of the feed, and disagreement between them is informative rather
    than annoying.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stage-3-roster">3. Assemble a Roster</h2>
  <p>
    Before writing code, build the team. Three agents is usually the right
    starting number:
  </p>
  <ul class="fields">
    <li>
      <strong>An architect</strong>
      <span>High effort, read-only. Investigates, plans, writes specifications.
      Cannot break anything, which is exactly what you want from the agent whose
      job is thinking.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A builder</strong>
      <span>Implements against a specification. Instructed to follow existing
      patterns and to say so when the spec is ambiguous rather than guessing.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A reviewer</strong>
      <span>Read-only, maximum effort, explicitly instructed to be adversarial.
      Its job is finding what is wrong, not being agreeable.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Give them names and personalities you enjoy. This sounds frivolous and is
    not: you will tag these agents dozens of times a day, and a roster you like
    working with is a roster you actually use well. Knowing that
    <em>@scout investigates</em> and <em>@grump reviews</em> is a faster thought
    than recalling which configuration profile you meant.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stage-4-document">4. Write It Down</h2>
  <p>
    Tag the architect and ask for a PRD: problem, scope, constraints, acceptance
    criteria. It replies with a knowledge page as an artifact.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read it properly. This is the cheapest point at which to catch a
    misunderstanding — a wrong assumption here becomes twelve wrong issues later.
    Iterate in the thread until it is right, then heart the version you agreed
    on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stage-5-issues">5. File the Issues</h2>
  <p>
    Reply in the same thread, tag an agent, and ask it to break the PRD into
    issues with dependencies. It has the specification as an artifact and the
    entire discussion as context, so it is not working from a summary.
  </p>
  <p>
    Review the breakdown before anything runs. Check that each issue is genuinely
    independent of its siblings and that the dependencies are real. Agents are
    good at decomposition and consistently over-optimistic about what can happen
    in parallel.
  </p>

  <h2 id="stage-6-execute">6. Tag and Review</h2>
  <p>Now the main loop, one issue at a time:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Post the issue</strong> in a thread, attached as an artifact, and tag the builder.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the reply</strong> and the diff it produced.</li>
    <li><strong>Tag the reviewer</strong> in the same thread to tear it apart.</li>
    <li><strong>Reply to the builder</strong> with what the review found, if anything. It has both the original work and the critique.</li>
    <li><strong>Commit</strong> when you are satisfied, and close the issue.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Once the shape is proven on a few issues, you can move faster — several
    independent issues in parallel threads, or an
    <a href="/docs/conductor/">epic with an execution chain</a> when the ordering
    is genuinely known. Earn that speed rather than starting with it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-this-works">Why This Works</h2>
  <p>
    Every stage produces something durable: a discussion, a specification, a set
    of issues, a reviewed diff. Nothing depends on remembering what was decided,
    because the decision is attached to the work.
  </p>
  <p>
    And nothing was handed over blind. Each round is small enough that a bad
    answer costs you one reply, not a week — which is what makes it reasonable to
    move quickly.
  </p>
  <p>
    The collaboration is the substance, not the packaging. Two agents disagreeing
    about storage, a reviewer catching what the builder missed, a specification
    that survived an argument — that is the process producing better software
    than any single agent handed the whole job would have.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tips">Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Commit before every tag.</strong> A clean tree makes every agent change trivially reversible.</li>
    <li><strong>Let the reviewer be genuinely harsh.</strong> An agreeable reviewer is worse than no reviewer, because you will trust it.</li>
    <li><strong>Write down what you rejected,</strong> not just what you chose. Otherwise an agent proposes it again in a month.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep threads to one subject.</strong> When you write "unrelated, but…", start a new post.</li>
    <li><strong>Refine instructions as you go.</strong> The third time you correct the same mistake, put the correction in the agent's instructions or a <a href="/docs/skills/">skill</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsPage>
